---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = "/guides/migration";
const title = "Shopify email platform migration";
const desc = "Transfer state without duplicating journeys for Shopify lifecycle email with explicit owners, proposed exercises and reversible decisions.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can both systems stay connected?",
    "answer": "Yes where their roles are explicit. Give each live journey a single sending owner and test that background sync does not enroll duplicate equivalents."
  },
  {
    "question": "When can the incumbent be cancelled?",
    "answer": "After required exports, outstanding journeys, integration ownership, rollback needs and contractual terms have been reconciled."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is there a universal warmup schedule?",
    "answer": "No. Agree a plan with the sending provider for the actual domain, audience and volume, and adjust from observed receiver feedback."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">Operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Transfer state without duplicating journeys</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Transfer state without duplicating journeys for Shopify lifecycle email with explicit owners, proposed exercises and reversible decisions.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl"><h2>Write the reason and the exit conditions</h2><p>Document the concrete limitation in the incumbent and the required behavior in the replacement. Compare repair with migration, including labor, overlapping subscriptions, connectors and training. Record the quote unit, currency and billing period before making any financial comparison. A cheaper headline plan cannot establish a payback guarantee.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl"><h2>Inventory integrations before exports</h2><p>Map Shopify, capture forms, SMS, reviews, loyalty, subscriptions, support and analytics to their actual owners. Identify which service sends essential order notices. Preserve that service until a separately reviewed replacement is ready. Record credentials ownership without putting secrets in the migration sheet.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl"><h2>Preserve permission and suppression state</h2><p>Export permission source and timestamp, withdrawals, complaints and relevant delivery suppressions using supported tools. Map the destination fields and their import behavior. Load exclusion state before eligible marketing audiences and verify it remains excluded. Missing permission evidence belongs in quarantine for review; deletion of the record is not a substitute for preserving suppression.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl"><h2>Map identity and historical objects</h2><p>Define stable customer, order, checkout and variant identifiers, with treatment for guests, changed addresses and merged accounts. Separate historical records from live triggers. Keep original event times and import times distinguishable. Reconcile counts by category and inspect records at boundaries; matching totals can hide incorrect field mapping.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl"><h2>Translate definitions rather than names</h2><p>Rebuild dynamic segments from their predicates, including timezone, refund, subscription and support rules. A CSV is a membership snapshot, not a future segment definition. For each flow, compare triggers, delays, exclusions, offer promises and terminal states. Keep a list of features that need redesign instead of assuming equivalent labels behave identically.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl"><h2>Handle events during the move</h2><p>Define a snapshot boundary and a change ledger for subsequent orders, withdrawals and other eligibility changes. Ask the integration owner how retries, duplicates and delayed delivery are handled. Reconcile updates before enabling destination flows. If the destination cannot honor a newly withdrawn permission promptly, hold affected promotional queues.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl"><h2>Rehearse without dual sending</h2><p>Run destination logic in a non-sending mode against representative permitted test records while the incumbent retains delivery ownership. Compare decisions, not two emails sent to a shopper. Exercise purchase during delay, stale import after withdrawal, repeated event and cart replacement. This is a proposed rehearsal with expected outcomes, not a report of a successful merchant migration.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl"><h2>Cut over each owned journey</h2><p>Record the exact incumbent branch, pending work, stop action, new enrollment boundary and responsible operator. Pause the old equivalent and resolve its queued messages before enabling the new one. For already-started journeys, explicitly finish in the old system or transfer remaining steps with a durable sent-message record. Do not restart everyone at step one.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl"><h2>Observe and roll back safely</h2><p>Verify provider setup and use a sending plan based on audience evidence and receiver feedback. Watch delivery errors, complaints, unexpected enrollment, duplicate sends and permission changes. Pause expansion on confirmed defects. A rollback must import destination withdrawals and sent-message history before resuming old logic, or it can repeat both sends and consent mistakes.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12"><div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl"><h2>Archive and close the comparison</h2><p>Export agreed performance definitions, message versions, permission mappings, reconciliation logs and unresolved issues before cancelling access. Set retention around business and applicable obligations, rather than an arbitrary universal archive duration. Compare reports using consistent windows, currency, refunds and cohort maturity; changed tracking prevents a simple dashboard-to-dashboard claim of uplift.</p></div></section>
  <section class="container pb-12 prose-shopify"><h2>Integration evidence to request</h2><p>For a custom webhook integration, Shopify documents delivery verification and duplicate handling. Ask the integration owner to show how those controls connect to journey state; webhook verification alone does not establish permission to send.</p><p><a class="link" href="https://shopify.dev/docs/apps/build/webhooks/verify-deliveries">Shopify: verify webhook deliveries</a> (reviewed 25 November 2026).</p></section>
  <nav class="container pb-12 flex flex-wrap gap-4" aria-label="Related operating guidance">
    <a class="link" href="/guides/consent-compliance">Consent compliance</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/deliverability">Deliverability</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/discount-discipline">Discount discipline</a>
    <a class="link" href="/guides/revenue-attribution">Revenue attribution</a>
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">Use-case playbooks</a>
    <a class="link" href="/for">Store-type guides</a>
  </nav>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
